  "requirement_items": [
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": "",
      "text": "APS includes all the necessary hardware, software, and interfaces (to the TCS; and M1, M2, and M3 control systems) required to accomplish the alignment tasks defined above.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": "",
      "text": "APS will have an acquisition camera with a 1 to 2 arcminute field of view which can be used for telescope pointing, acquisition, and tracking tests.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": "",
      "text": "APS will also provide an optical port where a guider camera and a low order wavefront sensor can be placed in order to test its performance and to validate the active optics control algorithms.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.12",
      "label": "",
      "text": "APS will provide an expert user GUI.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "[REQ-1-OAD-0173]",
      "text": "The Telescope Control system decomposition element is defined as follows:",
      "id": "REQ-1-OAD-0173"
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The TCS is responsible for the coordination and control of the various subsystems that comprise the telescope system.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The TCS primarily consists of software and the associated off the shelf computer hardware necessary to perform the following functions.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The TCS consists of a Sequencer and Status/Alarm Monitor, a Pointing Kernel, a Corrections Module, and several adaptors.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The Sequencer and the Status/Alarm Monitor controls and coordinates the telescope systems based on commands received from the Observatory Control System (OCS) and expert user interfaces.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The TCS Sequencer and Status/Alarm Monitor provides high level control of the mount, M1, M2, M3, and the enclosure (cap, base, shutter, vents).",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The enclosure vents will be controllable individually or via pre-set configurations; the design will provide the hooks enabling future automated control of vent configurations based on environmental conditions",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The TCS pointing kernel converts target positions (right ascension and declination) into pointing and tracking demands in the appropriate coordinate system for use by the telescope mount; instrument and AO WFS probes, atmospheric dispersion correctors, rotators; and the enclosure cap and base.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "In seeing limited operation, the correction module receives and processes focus, tip/tilt, coma and low radial order corrections from an instrument WFS that have been reconstructed and rotated into telescope mount, M1, and M2 coordinates.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "In diffraction limited mode (AO) the corrections are based on an offload of the time averaged position of the AO tip/tilt stage and the DM; up to 100 modes can be offloaded in this configuration.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The corrections module also processes data from the Global Metrology System for use by the M1, M2 and M3 systems.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The corrections module is also responsible for the creation and management of the M1, M2, and M3 rigid body and M1 shape LUTs.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The TCS contains adaptors to handle differences between vendor and commercially supplied software systems and the core observatory software systems.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "There will be adaptors for the M2, M3, Enclosure, Structure and Engineering Sensor systems.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.13",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The TCS includes an expert user GUI.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.14",
      "label": "[REQ-1-OAD-0179]",
      "text": "The M1 Control system decomposition element is defined as follows:",
      "id": "REQ-1-OAD-0179"
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.14",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The M1 Control System (M1CS) is responsible for maintaining the overall shape of the segmented M1 mirror despite structural deformations caused by temperature and gravity and disturbances from wind and vibrations (observatory generated and seismic).",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.14",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The M1 set-points are determined from measurements made with the APS.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.14",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The M1CS is a distributed control system that includes actuators for 492 segments, sensors for 574 segments (includes sensors for spare segments), electronics mounted and distributed on the telescope mirror cell, telescope and segment mounted cabling, telescope mounted power supplies, a communications bus, control software, and associated computer processing hardware.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.14",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The M1CS also controls the M1 warping harness actuators and reads the warping harness strain gauges.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.14",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The design and packaging of the electronics mounted on the mirror cell will limit the amount of heat released into the local environment.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.14",
      "label": "",
      "text": "Installation and calibration equipment required to mount the sensors to the segments is included.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.14",
      "label": "",
      "text": "Test sets will be provided to enable quick and efficient lab bench testing of PCBs, actuators, and sensors.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.14",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The M1CS software will include comprehensive diagnostic capability and an expert user GUI.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.15",
      "label": "[REQ-1-OAD-0185]",
      "text": "The Observatory Safety system decomposition element is defined as follows:",
      "id": "REQ-1-OAD-0185"
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.15",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The Observatory Safety System (OSS) contributes to the enforcement of safe conditions throughout the summit facility by continuously monitoring the state of connected equipment, systems and sensors and taking appropriate action as soon as an unsafe condition is detected.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.15",
      "label": "",
      "text": "It is independent from and supplementary to any safety systems and functionality that is contained within individual subsystems.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.15",
      "label": "",
      "text": "Based on one or more Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs), it will interface with connected subsystems via a dedicated safety rated fieldbus based on EtherNet/IP; monitor interlock requests and possibly a defined subset of additional signals from all connected subsystems; monitor the emergency stop switches located throughout the summit facility; manage safety interlock enforcement between sub-systems; provide a user interface that provides fault and interlock reporting and reset capabilities: communicate the safety state of all connected subsystems to, at a minimum, the Data Management System (DMS) & Executive Software (ESW).",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.15",
      "label": "",
      "text": "The OSS includes the Global Safety Controller (GSC), remote I/O modules, the fieldbus network and associated networking components such as switches, a Human-Machine Interface (HMI), racks, enclosures, power supplies, network cabling, and all associated PLC software.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.15",
      "label": "",
      "text": "It does not include the Local Safety Controllers, fire suppression systems, emergency lighting or the individual emergency stop buttons' wiring and mounting hardware.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.15",
      "label": "",
      "text": "These are the responsibility of the individual subsystems.",
      "id": null
    },
    {
      "section": "2.2.1.16",
      "label": "[REQ-1-OAD-0188]",
      "text": "The Engineering Sensor system decomposition element is defined as follows:",
      "id": "REQ-1-OAD-0188"
    }
  ],
